          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "224.0.0.0/4 = the old Class D, 224-239",
            "198.18.0.0/15 keeps lab test traffic off real prefixes",
            "OSPF hellos use multicast 224.0.0.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "import ipaddress as ip",
            "a = ip.ip_address('224.0.0.5')",
            "print(a.is_multicast)  # True"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Memorize These",
          "items": [
            "Private: 10/8, 172.16/12, 192.168/16",
            "Loopback 127/8, link-local 169.254/16",
            "CGNAT 100.64/10",
            "Docs: 192.0.2, 198.51.100, 203.0.113 /24"
          ],
          "ink": "accent"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "240.0.0.0/4 reserved and experimental",
            "0.0.0.0/8 means 'this network'",
            "255.255.255.255 is the limited broadcast"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Route Tables & Longest Prefix Match",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Pick An Odd /16",
          "text": "Everyone grabs 10.0.0.0/8, so VPNs and mergers collide. Choosing something like 10.147.0.0/16 inside it removes most of the overlap pain."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. What A Route Entry Is",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "A destination prefix mapped to next-hop plus interface",
            "Match test: destination AND entry_mask == entry_network"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "dest = 0x0A010207        # 10.1.2.7",
            "mask = 0xFFFFFF00        # /24",
            "print(hex(dest & mask))  # 0xa010200"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Longest Prefix Match",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Among all matching entries, most network bits wins",
            "Specificity is decided BEFORE distance or metric",
            "A /32 from a lowly static beats a /24 from OSPF",
            "A /32 host route overrides everything for that address"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Entry",
            "Matches 10.1.2.7?",
            "Bits"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "0.0.0.0/0",
              "Yes",
              "0"
            ],
            [
              "10.0.0.0/8",
              "Yes",
              "8"
            ],
            [
              "10.1.0.0/16",
              "Yes",
              "16"
            ],
            [
              "10.1.2.0/24",
              "Yes — **wins**",
              "24"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 3
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "import ipaddress as ip",
            "d = ip.ip_address('10.1.2.7')",
            "t = ['0.0.0.0/0', '10.1.2.0/24']",
            "ns = [ip.ip_network(x) for x in t]",
            "hit = [n for n in ns if d in n]",
            "max(hit,key=lambda n:n.prefixlen)"
          ],
          "note": "returns 10.1.2.0/24"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Fallback And Holes",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "0.0.0.0/0 matches all, so it ==loses== to any other match",
            "Punch a hole: send 10/8 one way, 10.1.2.0/24 another",
            "No match and no default -> drop + ICMP net unreachable"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Routes Are Not ACLs",
          "text": "Route table order never matters — longest match wins. ACLs and firewall rules are first-match, top-down, so order is everything."
        }
      ]
    }
